WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.277
A cleaned value should be easy to explain.

00:00:03.277 --> 00:00:09.982
Consider a customer name stored as Jane Smith with several extra spaces around it.

00:00:09.982 --> 00:00:16.287
A cleaned version can remove those spaces and use consistent capitalization.

00:00:16.287 --> 00:00:20.117
The same idea applies to an email address.

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:25.317
We can remove surrounding spaces and convert the text to lowercase.

00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:30.438
For a phone number, we may remove punctuation and keep only the digits.

00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:36.675
These are useful analytical representations, but the original values still matter.

00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:45.355
If someone asks how a cleaned value was produced, we should be able to point back to the source and describe the

00:00:45.355 --> 00:00:49.435
transformation. This is called preserving lineage or provenance.

00:00:49.435 --> 00:00:54.930
It means keeping enough information to reproduce and audit the cleaning process.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:01:01.215
When possible, think in terms of two layers: what the source actually contained,

00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:04.505
and what representation we created for analysis.
